/* Explicación gramatical de cada ítem de la Part 4 (Reading and Use of English),
   que se muestra en la corrección. Clave: tema:nivel:índice. */
window.RUOE_WHY = {
"comparatives:b2:0": "Negative equality: <b>not as + adjective + as</b>. ‘Taller than’ reverses to ‘Peter is not as tall as John’: the subjects swap and the comparison turns negative. Avoid ‘not as taller as’ — between as…as the adjective stays in its base form.",
"comparatives:b2:1": "Superlative + present perfect: <b>the + superlative + noun + I’ve ever seen</b>. ‘Never seen such a beautiful house’ means no other house is more beautiful, so this one is ‘the most beautiful house’. Avoid ‘the most beautifulest’ — long adjectives take most, not -est.",
"comparatives:b2:10": "Superlative: <b>the + superlative + in the class</b>. ‘No one is as smart as Emma’ means nobody exceeds her, so she is the smartest. Use in (not of) with a group like the class. Avoid ‘the most smart’ — short adjectives take -est.",
"comparatives:b2:11": "Opposite adjective with negative equality: <b>not as + adjective + as</b>. ‘Longer than I expected’ means I expected it to be shorter, so we write ‘was not as short as I expected’. Avoid ‘not as shorter as’ — keep the base adjective.",
"comparatives:b2:12": "<b>too + adjective + to + infinitive</b>. ‘So tired that he can’t walk’ becomes ‘too tired to walk’: too already carries the negative result, so no ‘not’ is needed. Avoid ‘too tired to not walk’ or ‘too tired for walk’.",
"comparatives:b2:13": "Negative perfect: <b>never + past participle + such a(n) + adjective + noun</b>. ‘The most delicious meal I’ve ever eaten’ means no meal was better, so ‘I have never eaten such a delicious meal’. Avoid ‘such delicious meal’ — a singular countable noun needs a.",
"comparatives:b2:14": "Negative comparative: <b>no + noun + less expensive than</b>. ‘The cheapest restaurant’ means every other restaurant costs more, so none is cheaper. The gap is filled with ‘restaurant less expensive than’. Avoid ‘less cheaper than’ — less takes the base adjective.",
"comparatives:b2:15": "<b>too little + uncountable noun</b>. ‘Don’t have enough time’ and ‘too little time’ both mean the amount is insufficient. Time is uncountable, so use little, not few. Avoid ‘too few time’.",
"comparatives:b2:16": "Equality: <b>as + adjective + as</b>. ‘The same height as’ expresses equality, shown with the base adjective between two as’s. Avoid ‘as taller as’ — a comparative form is never used inside as…as.",
"comparatives:b2:17": "<b>so + adverb + that</b> result clause. ‘Because it was raining hard’ becomes ‘raining so hard that we stayed indoors’: the cause now leads to its consequence. Avoid ‘so hardly’ — hard is both adjective and adverb, but hardly means almost not.",
"comparatives:b2:18": "Superlative + present perfect: <b>the worst film + we have ever watched</b>. ‘Never watched a worse film’ means no film is worse, so ‘the worst film’ with ever in the relative clause. Avoid adding never here — ever, not never, follows a superlative.",
"comparatives:b2:19": "Double comparative: <b>The more I study, the more confident I feel</b>. ‘As I study more, I feel more confident’ shows two changes growing together, so each clause begins with the + comparative. Avoid ‘The more I study, I feel more confident’ — the second the is needed.",
"comparatives:b2:2": "<b>not + adjective + enough</b>: enough follows the adjective. ‘Too hot to drink’ and ‘not cool enough to drink’ both mean the temperature stops you drinking, so the adjective flips to its opposite. Avoid ‘enough cool’ — enough comes after the adjective.",
"comparatives:b2:3": "<b>such + a/an + adjective + noun</b> gives the same result clause as so + adjective. ‘The book was so boring’ becomes ‘such a boring book’ because such needs a noun. Avoid ‘such boring’ without a noun, or ‘such a boring’ on its own.",
"comparatives:b2:4": "Comparative used instead of a superlative: <b>everyone else is better than him</b>. If he is the worst, all the others are better, so the subject changes to ‘everyone else’. Avoid ‘more good than’ — good has the irregular comparative better.",
"comparatives:b2:5": "<b>did not have enough + noun</b> replaces too + adjective + to-infinitive. ‘Too expensive for me to buy’ means my money was insufficient, so the focus moves from the car to the money. Avoid ‘enough of money’ — enough goes directly before the noun.",
"comparatives:b2:6": "Negative equality with a verb: <b>doesn’t + verb + as + adverb + as</b>. ‘Sarah speaks French better’ means Mark is less skilled, so we need the adverb well (not good) after speak French. Avoid ‘doesn’t speak as good as’ — a verb needs an adverb.",
"comparatives:b2:7": "Double comparative: <b>the + comparative, the + comparative</b>. ‘As you get older, you become wiser’ shows two changes happening together, so both clauses begin with the. Avoid ‘the older you get, you become wiser’ — the second the is needed.",
"comparatives:b2:8": "Intensified negative comparison: <b>not nearly as + adjective + as</b>. ‘Much easier than the last one’ means the last one was far harder, so the subjects swap and the adjective flips to difficult. Avoid ‘nearly not as’ — not comes before nearly.",
"comparatives:b2:9": "<b>too few + plural countable noun</b>. ‘There aren’t enough chairs’ means the number is insufficient, which is exactly what too few says. Avoid ‘too little chairs’ — chairs is countable, so use few, not little.",
"comparatives:c1:0": "Strong negative comparison: <b>nowhere near as + adjective + as</b>. ‘Much better than last year’s’ reverses so that last year’s results are now the subject, and nowhere near intensifies not as…as. Avoid ‘nowhere near as better as’ — use the base adjective good.",
"comparatives:c1:1": "Phrasal-prepositional verb: <b>live up to + noun phrase</b>. ‘Expected the hotel to be much better’ means reality fell short of hopes, so ‘didn’t live up to my expectations’. Avoid ‘live up my expectations’ — to is required.",
"comparatives:c1:2": "Double comparative: <b>the more… the less I like</b>. ‘Like the idea less and less’ becomes ‘the less I like the idea’; the second clause starts with the + comparative, then subject + verb. Avoid ‘the less I am liking’ — like is a stative verb.",
"comparatives:c1:3": "Negative subject: <b>Nobody else in + group + is as… as</b>. ‘By far the most talented’ means no other singer equals her, so we use nobody else in the choir. Avoid ‘nobody of the choir’ — use in with a group.",
"comparatives:c1:4": "Superlative: <b>the most + adjective + noun</b>. ‘Never been so embarrassed’ means no other moment was more embarrassing, so ‘the most embarrassing moment’. Avoid ‘the most embarrassed moment’ — the moment causes the feeling, so we need -ing, not -ed.",
"comparatives:c1:5": "Emphatic equality: <b>every bit as + adjective + as</b>. ‘Just as successful as’ and ‘every bit as successful as’ both stress that the two books were identical in degree. Avoid ‘every bit so successful as’ — only as…as fits this expression.",
"comparatives:c1:6": "Negative equality with a quantifier: <b>not nearly as many + plural noun + as</b>. ‘Far fewer people’ means a much smaller number. People is plural countable, so use many. Avoid ‘not nearly as much people’.",
"comparatives:c1:7": "Preference with gerunds: <b>prefer + -ing + to + -ing</b>. ‘I’d rather walk than take the bus’ means ‘I prefer walking to taking the bus’. Here to is a preposition, so both verbs take -ing. Avoid ‘prefer walking than taking’.",
"conditionals:b2:0": "Wish about a past regret: <b>wish + past perfect</b>. ‘I regret not studying harder’ becomes ‘I wish I had studied harder’ — a past perfect for something that did not happen. Avoid ‘I wish I studied’, which refers to the present, or ‘would study’.",
"conditionals:b2:1": "Condition word: <b>as long as / so long as + present simple</b>. It means ‘only if’, so ‘if you pay me’ becomes ‘as long as you pay me’, with the same tense. Avoid ‘as long as you will pay’ — no ‘will’ in the condition clause.",
"conditionals:b2:10": "Giving advice with a second conditional: <b>If I were you, I would + base verb</b>. ‘I advise you to speak’ becomes ‘If I were you, I would speak’. Use ‘were’ for all persons in this fixed phrase. Avoid ‘If I would be you’.",
"conditionals:b2:11": "Third conditional with a past regret: <b>If + past perfect, would not have + past participle</b>. The real facts were that she took no umbrella and got wet, so ‘If she had taken’ reverses the first part. Avoid ‘If she would have taken’.",
"conditionals:b2:12": "Wish about a past regret: <b>wish + past perfect</b>. ‘I'm sorry I offended you’ becomes ‘I wish I had not offended you’, since the action is already finished. Avoid ‘I wish I didn't offend’, which would refer to the present.",
"conditionals:b2:13": "‘Unless’ replaces ‘if … not’, so the verb is affirmative: <b>unless + present simple</b>. ‘If it doesn't rain’ becomes ‘unless it rains’. Avoid ‘unless it doesn't rain’ — that duplicates the negative meaning.",
"conditionals:b2:14": "Preference with a different subject: <b>would rather + subject + past simple</b>. ‘I'd prefer you to wear a suit’ becomes ‘I would rather you wore a suit’, with a past form that is unreal. Avoid ‘rather you to wear’ or ‘rather you wear’.",
"conditionals:b2:15": "Third conditional: <b>If + past perfect, would not have + past participle</b>. ‘He didn't know the answer, so he failed’ becomes ‘If he had known the answer, he wouldn't have failed’. Avoid ‘If he would have known’ or ‘knew’.",
"conditionals:b2:16": "Wish for annoying behaviour: <b>wish + subject + would + base verb</b>. ‘Stop making so much noise!’ becomes ‘I wish you would stop’, expressing irritation with the present habit. Avoid ‘I wish you stopped’ or ‘wish you to stop’.",
"conditionals:b2:17": "‘It's time’ + subject + past simple: <b>It's time you started</b>. The past tense is unreal: you should start now. Here ‘you started looking’ keeps the sense of ‘ought to’. Avoid ‘It's time you start’ or ‘time you to start’.",
"conditionals:b2:18": "Condition word: <b>as long as / so long as + present simple</b>. It means ‘only if’, replacing ‘only if it is sunny’, and the verb stays in the present. Avoid ‘as long as it will be sunny’ — no ‘will’ in a time or condition clause.",
"conditionals:b2:19": "Wish-like regret with ‘If only’: <b>If only + past perfect</b>. ‘I regret buying’ becomes ‘If only I had not bought’, expressing regret about something done in the past. Avoid ‘If only I didn't buy’, which refers to the present.",
"conditionals:b2:2": "‘Unless’ means ‘if … not’, so the clause after it is affirmative: <b>unless + present simple</b>. ‘If they don't invite him’ becomes ‘unless they invite him’. Avoid ‘unless they don't invite’ — that is a double negative.",
"conditionals:b2:3": "Wish-like regret with ‘If only’: <b>If only + past simple/could</b>. A present pity (‘I can't speak French’) is expressed as ‘If only I could speak French’, with past forms for an unreal present. Avoid ‘If only I can speak’.",
"conditionals:b2:4": "Preference with a different subject: <b>would rather + subject + past simple</b>. ‘I'd rather you did not smoke’ refers to the present or future, but the verb is past because it is unreal. Avoid ‘I'd rather you don't smoke’ in exams, or ‘rather you to smoke’.",
"conditionals:b2:5": "Mixed conditional: <b>If + past perfect, would + base verb</b>. A past action (going to bed late) has a present result (she is tired), so the result clause uses ‘wouldn't be’. Avoid ‘wouldn't have been tired’, which refers only to the past.",
"conditionals:b2:6": "Second conditional: <b>If + past simple, would + base verb</b>. The unreal present (he doesn't study) leads to ‘If he studied, he would pass’. Avoid ‘he would passed’ — after ‘would’ use the base form.",
"conditionals:b2:7": "Third conditional: <b>If + past perfect, would have + past participle</b>. ‘We didn't bring a map’ becomes ‘If we had brought a map’ — the opposite of what happened. Avoid ‘If we would have brought’ — no ‘would’ in the if-clause.",
"conditionals:b2:8": "Condition word: <b>provided (that) + present simple</b>. It means ‘only if’, so ‘but you must drive carefully’ becomes ‘provided you drive carefully’, with a present form for a future idea. Avoid ‘provided you will drive’.",
"conditionals:b2:9": "‘It's time’ + subject + past simple: <b>It is time + we went</b>. The past tense is unreal, meaning we should go now but have not yet. Avoid ‘It is time we go’ in exams; ‘time to go’ takes an infinitive, but here a new subject is used.",
"conditionals:c1:0": "Formal inverted conditional: <b>Should + subject + base verb</b>. It replaces ‘If you happen to need’ and suggests a small chance. Avoid ‘Should you will need’ — after the subject use the base form, with no ‘to’ or ‘will’.",
"conditionals:c1:1": "Inverted third conditional: <b>Had + subject + past participle</b>. ‘If I had known’ becomes ‘Had I known’ — the auxiliary ‘had’ moves before the subject and ‘if’ is dropped. Avoid ‘Had I knew’ or ‘If had I known’.",
"conditionals:c1:2": "Negative form of ‘but for’: <b>If it had not been for + noun</b>. ‘Without your support’ becomes ‘If it had not been for your support’ — a third conditional that means ‘because you did help’. Avoid ‘If it wasn't for’ in a past-perfect context.",
"conditionals:c1:3": "‘But for’ = ‘without’ + noun, used with a third conditional: <b>But for + noun, would have + past participle</b>. ‘We only won because our goalkeeper was brilliant’ becomes ‘But for our brilliant goalkeeper, we would have lost’. Avoid a clause after ‘but for’.",
"conditionals:c1:4": "Preference with ‘sooner’: <b>would sooner + subject + past simple</b>. It works like ‘would rather’ and uses the past tense for an unreal wish about now or the future. Avoid ‘sooner you didn't to mention’ — no ‘to’.",
"conditionals:c1:5": "‘Supposing’ works like ‘if’ in a hypothetical question: <b>supposing + past simple</b>, followed by ‘would’. ‘Imagine you won’ becomes ‘Supposing you won’. ‘Were to’ makes it more unlikely. Avoid ‘supposing you will win’.",
"conditionals:c1:6": "Criticism of a past action: <b>wish + subject + past perfect</b>. ‘You should have told me’ becomes ‘I wish you had told me’ — the regret is about the past. Avoid ‘I wish you told’ (present) or ‘would have told’.",
"conditionals:c1:7": "‘It's high time’ + past simple: <b>It's high time + subject + past simple</b>. It means the action is overdue; the past tense is unreal. ‘Take action on/against pollution’ must be in the past: ‘took’. Avoid ‘high time the government take’.",
"fixedphrases:c1:0": "<b>In charge of</b> = responsible for. The adjective responsible is replaced by this prepositional phrase, followed by a noun: ‘in charge of the new project’. Avoid ‘in charge for’ or ‘in the charge of’ — the preposition is of and there is no article.",
"fixedphrases:c1:1": "<b>With a view to</b> + -ing = with the aim of. Here <b>to</b> is a preposition, so a gerund follows: ‘with a view to improving’. Avoid ‘with a view to improve’ — an infinitive is incorrect after this phrase.",
"fixedphrases:c1:10": "<b>Come as a surprise / shock to</b> sb. The subject changes from the person (‘he was surprised’) to the news: ‘The news came as a surprise to him’. Use object pronoun him after to. Avoid ‘came like a surprise’ — the fixed phrase uses as.",
"fixedphrases:c1:11": "<b>Be generally considered / thought / believed + to be</b>. This is an impersonal passive: ‘most people agree that she is’ becomes ‘she is generally considered to be the best’. Avoid ‘is generally considered as’ — consider takes ‘to be’, while regarded takes ‘as’.",
"fixedphrases:c1:2": "<b>At odds</b> (with sb / over or about sth) = in disagreement. ‘Disagree about the budget’ becomes ‘are at odds over the budget’. The verb be (are) is needed because the managers are plural. Avoid ‘at odd’ — the noun is plural.",
"fixedphrases:c1:3": "<b>With the exception of</b> + noun = except. It introduces the excluded person at the start of the sentence, followed by a comma: ‘With the exception of Jack, everyone…’. Avoid ‘with exception of’ or ‘with the exception that’ — the article and of are needed.",
"fixedphrases:c1:4": "<b>Unaware of</b> + noun = not knowing about something. ‘Didn’t realise the danger’ becomes the adjective phrase ‘was unaware of the danger he was in’. Avoid ‘unaware about’ or ‘unaware that of’ — the preposition must be <b>of</b>.",
"fixedphrases:c1:5": "<b>Come into effect</b> / <b>take effect</b> = start to apply (laws, rules). ‘Apply from’ becomes ‘come into effect next Monday’ or ‘take effect from next Monday’. Avoid ‘come in effect’ — you need into. Take effect has no into.",
"fixedphrases:c1:6": "<b>Have no doubt (that)</b> + clause = be completely sure. ‘I’m sure you will succeed’ becomes ‘I have no doubt (that) you will succeed’. Avoid ‘I have not doubt’ or ‘I have no doubt of you will’ — use no before doubt, then a clause.",
"fixedphrases:c1:7": "<b>Make no mention of</b> sth = not say anything about it. The negative is in the noun phrase, so the verb is positive: ‘made no mention of her plans’. Avoid ‘didn’t make no mention’ (double negative) and ‘mention about’ — the preposition is of.",
"fixedphrases:c1:8": "<b>On account of</b> + noun = because of. It is a formal prepositional phrase, followed by a noun phrase: ‘on account of the storm’. Avoid ‘on account of that’ + clause (it needs a noun), and avoid ‘in account of’ or ‘by account of’.",
"fixedphrases:c1:9": "<b>Have no interest in</b> + noun / -ing = not be interested in. The negative adjective ‘not interested’ becomes the noun phrase ‘no interest in politics’. Avoid ‘have no interested in’ or ‘no interest on’ — the noun is interest and the preposition is in.",
"future:b2:0": "<b>Be going to</b> + infinitive expresses a plan or intention already decided. ‘Plan to visit’ becomes ‘am going to visit’, with the verb am agreeing with I. Avoid ‘am going to visiting’ or ‘going to visit’ without am.",
"future:b2:1": "<b>Be due to</b> + infinitive = be scheduled to happen. It replaces the timetable present simple (‘starts’): ‘is due to start’. Avoid ‘is due start’ without to, or ‘due to’ + verb with no subject-verb agreement (film = is).",
"future:b2:2": "<b>Be bound to</b> + infinitive = be certain to happen (‘I’m sure he’ll…’). The subject becomes he, and the verb is bound agrees with him: ‘is bound to be late’. Avoid ‘is bound being’ — it must be followed by the infinitive.",
"future:b2:3": "<b>Probably</b> goes between will and the main verb: subject + will + probably + infinitive. ‘It’s very likely that it will rain’ becomes ‘It will probably rain’. Avoid ‘probably will’ in affirmative statements — it is usually placed after will.",
"future:b2:4": "<b>Be about to</b> + infinitive = be on the point of doing something, very near future. It replaces ‘going to … in a moment’: ‘is about to leave’. Avoid adding a time expression like ‘tomorrow’ — about to already means immediately.",
"future:b2:5": "<b>Future perfect</b>: will have + past participle, for an action completed before a future time. ‘Before you arrive’ becomes ‘by the time you arrive’, so you need ‘will have finished’. Avoid ‘will finish’ — it loses the idea of completion before.",
"future:b2:6": "<b>Future continuous</b>: will be + -ing, for an action in progress at a specific future time (nine o’clock). ‘In the middle of my exam’ becomes ‘will be doing my exam’. Avoid ‘will do’, which does not show the action is already in progress.",
"future:b2:7": "<b>Be likely to</b> + infinitive. ‘There’s a good chance that she will get’ becomes ‘she is likely to get’; the subject changes from ‘there’ to she. Avoid ‘is likely getting’ or ‘is likely that’ with a personal subject — use the infinitive.",
"future:b2:8": "<b>Present continuous for arrangements</b>: be + -ing, with a future time. ‘We have arranged to meet’ becomes ‘We are meeting Sarah’. Use it for fixed plans with another person. Avoid ‘will meet’ — it sounds like a spontaneous decision, not an arrangement.",
"future:b2:9": "<b>There’s no chance of</b> + -ing. ‘Impossible that they will win’ becomes a noun phrase with a gerund: ‘no chance of them (or their) winning’. Avoid ‘no chance to win’ or ‘no chance of win’ — the preposition of must be followed by -ing.",
"future:c1:0": "<b>In all likelihood</b> = very probably. It is a fixed phrase replacing ‘in all probability’, and it comes at the start of the sentence, followed by a comma: ‘In all likelihood, prices will…’. Avoid ‘in all the likelihood’ or ‘at all likelihood’.",
"future:c1:1": "<b>Doubt</b> + that / whether / if + clause. ‘It’s unlikely that the government will change’ becomes the verb ‘I doubt that the government will change’. The doubt is in the verb, so no negative is needed. Avoid ‘I don’t doubt’ — it reverses the meaning.",
"future:c1:2": "<b>On the verge of</b> + noun / -ing = about to happen or do something. It replaces ‘about to begin’ and needs a gerund after of: ‘on the verge of beginning’. Avoid ‘on the verge to begin’ — the preposition of is followed by -ing.",
"future:c1:3": "<b>No chance whatsoever (that)</b> + clause. The emphatic ‘no way’ is expressed by no + noun + <b>whatsoever</b>, a postmodifier meaning ‘at all’: ‘no chance whatsoever she will agree’. Avoid ‘no any chance’ — no already makes the noun negative.",
"future:c1:4": "<b>Be certain to</b> + infinitive = will definitely happen. ‘Will definitely surprise’ becomes ‘are certain to surprise’; the plural subject ‘results’ needs are. Avoid ‘are certain surprise’ without to, or ‘certain that’ + infinitive.",
"future:c1:5": "<b>Be due to</b> + infinitive = be scheduled or expected to. The passive ‘is expected to announce’ becomes the active ‘is due to announce’. Avoid ‘is due announcing’ or ‘is due that he announces’ — it must be followed by a to-infinitive.",
"future:c1:6": "<b>Be set to</b> + infinitive = be expected or very likely to (in the near future). It replaces ‘predict that… will’: ‘is set to double’. The subject becomes the population. Avoid ‘is set doubling’ — use the infinitive after set to.",
"future:c1:7": "<b>The odds are in favour of</b> + noun / -ing = something is likely. ‘Very likely that they will win’ becomes ‘the odds are in favour of them winning’. Avoid ‘in favour to’ or ‘in favour of win’ — of is followed by a gerund.",
"inversion:c1:0": "<b>Inversion after a negative adverb</b>: Never + auxiliary + subject + past participle. With ‘Never’ at the start, past perfect ‘had seen’ becomes ‘had I seen’. The meaning stays the same, with more emphasis. Avoid ‘Never I had seen’ — the auxiliary must come first.",
"inversion:c1:1": "<b>No sooner + had + subject + past participle + than</b>. It replaces ‘as soon as’, with the earlier action in past perfect and the later one in past simple: ‘No sooner had I arrived than it started to rain’. Avoid ‘when’ instead of than.",
"inversion:c1:10": "<b>Pseudo-cleft sentence</b>: What + subject + verb + was + noun. The long noun clause ‘the thing that surprised me’ is shortened with what: ‘What surprised me most was his calmness’. Avoid ‘That surprised me most was’ — what replaces the thing that.",
"inversion:c1:11": "<b>So + adjective + be + subject + that</b>. ‘The weather was so bad that…’ becomes ‘So bad was the weather that…’. The adjective goes first for emphasis, and the verb be comes before the subject. Avoid ‘So bad the weather was’.",
"inversion:c1:2": "<b>At no time + auxiliary + subject + verb</b>. The negative phrase starts the sentence, so did + he + base verb follows: ‘At no time did he say sorry’. Avoid ‘At no time he said’ and avoid a double negative such as ‘didn’t say’.",
"inversion:c1:3": "<b>Not only + auxiliary + subject … but (also)</b>. The verb to be inverts after Not only: ‘Not only is she clever, but she is also very hard-working’. Avoid ‘Not only she is clever’ — only the first clause is inverted, not the second.",
"inversion:c1:4": "<b>Not until + clause + auxiliary + subject</b>. After ‘Not until the engineer explained it’, the main clause inverts: ‘did we understand’. The time clause itself keeps normal order. Avoid ‘we did understand’ or ‘Not until … we understood’.",
"inversion:c1:5": "<b>Under no circumstances + auxiliary + subject + base verb</b>. ‘Mustn’t … under any circumstances’ becomes ‘Under no circumstances must you open’. The negative is already in no, so do not add another. Avoid ‘must not you’ or ‘mustn’t’ after the inversion.",
"inversion:c1:6": "<b>Pseudo-cleft sentence</b>: What + subject + verb + is + noun. It puts the focus on the thing needed: ‘What I need is a long holiday’. Avoid ‘What I need are’ with a singular noun, and avoid ‘That I need is’ — the wh-word must be what.",
"inversion:c1:7": "<b>It-cleft</b>: It + was + focused item + that / which + clause. It stresses ‘Tom’s rudeness’ as the cause: ‘It was Tom’s rudeness that really annoyed me’. Avoid ‘It was Tom’s rudeness what annoyed me’ — use that or which, never what.",
"inversion:c1:8": "<b>Hardly + had + subject + past participle + when</b>. Like ‘no sooner … than’, it shows one past action right after another, with inversion after Hardly: ‘Hardly had I closed my eyes when…’. Avoid ‘Hardly I had closed’ or ‘than’ in place of when.",
"inversion:c1:9": "<b>Inversion after Rarely</b>: Rarely + auxiliary + subject + base verb. ‘Unusual to see’ becomes ‘Rarely do we see’ (do for present simple, with does after one/he). Avoid ‘Rarely we see’ — the auxiliary must come before the subject.",
"linkers:b2:0": "Concession: <b>in spite of + noun phrase</b>. ‘Although it was raining’ (clause) becomes ‘in spite of the rain’ (noun phrase), because a preposition needs a noun. Avoid ‘in spite of it was raining’ — a full clause cannot follow in spite of.",
"linkers:b2:1": "Cause: <b>due to + noun phrase</b>. ‘Because of the bad weather’ becomes ‘due to the bad weather’, and both are followed by a noun phrase. Avoid ‘due to the weather was bad’ — due to needs a noun, not a clause.",
"linkers:b2:10": "Concession: <b>despite + being</b>. ‘Even though she was late’ (clause) becomes ‘despite being late’ (preposition + gerund), keeping the same subject. Avoid ‘despite of being’ — despite never takes of (compare in spite of).",
"linkers:b2:11": "Purpose: <b>to avoid + -ing</b>. ‘So that I wouldn’t wake up late’ becomes ‘to avoid waking up late’, with avoid followed by a gerund. Avoid ‘to avoid to wake’ — avoid never takes an infinitive.",
"linkers:b2:12": "Cause and effect: <b>A strike resulted in + noun</b>. ‘The flight was delayed because of a strike’ turns the cause into the subject, so ‘resulted in the delay’. Avoid ‘resulted of’ or ‘resulted to’ — result in is followed by the effect.",
"linkers:b2:13": "<b>during + noun</b>. ‘While I was walking home’ (clause) becomes ‘during my walk home’ (noun phrase). Avoid ‘during I was walking’ — during is followed by a noun, while by a clause.",
"linkers:b2:14": "<b>despite the fact that + clause</b>. Despite takes a noun or gerund, so to keep a full clause (‘he was wearing’) we add the fact that. Avoid ‘despite he was wearing’ and ‘despite of the fact’.",
"linkers:b2:15": "Negative purpose: <b>so as not to + infinitive</b>. ‘So nobody would hear her’ becomes ‘so as not to be heard’, passive because the person who might hear is not the subject. Avoid ‘so as to not’ in an exam — put not directly before to.",
"linkers:b2:16": "Contrast: <b>even though + clause</b>. ‘But the ending was disappointing’ becomes ‘even though the ending was disappointing’, a subordinate clause. Avoid ‘even the ending’ — even alone does not link two clauses.",
"linkers:b2:17": "Reason: <b>the reason why + clause</b>. ‘We missed the train because…’ becomes ‘the reason why we missed the train’, with the cause as subject. Avoid ‘the reason because’ — why or that follows reason, not because.",
"linkers:b2:18": "Cause: <b>because of + noun phrase</b>. ‘Because it was raining heavily’ becomes ‘because of the heavy rain’; the adverb heavily turns into the adjective heavy. Avoid ‘because of it was raining’ — of needs a noun.",
"linkers:b2:19": "Addition: <b>and she also + verb</b>. ‘In addition to speaking’ becomes a second clause, ‘and she also speaks Italian’; also goes before the main verb. Avoid ‘and also she speaks’ — also normally comes after the subject.",
"linkers:b2:2": "Concession: <b>even though + clause</b>. ‘But he failed’ becomes ‘even though he studied hard’, a subordinate clause with its own subject and verb. Avoid ‘even he studied’ — even alone cannot introduce a clause; add though.",
"linkers:b2:3": "Precaution: <b>in case + subject + verb</b>. ‘Because it might rain’ becomes ‘in case it rained’; the past simple fits because the main verb is past. Avoid ‘in case it would rain’ — would does not follow in case.",
"linkers:b2:4": "Result: <b>such a + adjective + noun + that</b>. ‘The film was so boring that we left’ becomes ‘It was such a boring film that we left’, using the noun film. Avoid ‘such boring film’ — a singular countable noun needs a after such.",
"linkers:b2:5": "Result: <b>As a result, + clause</b>. ‘Consequently’ is a single linker that equals the longer phrase ‘as a result’, followed by a comma and a full clause. Avoid ‘as a result of they had to’ — of needs a noun, not a clause.",
"linkers:b2:6": "Purpose: <b>in order to + infinitive</b>. ‘To improve’ and ‘in order to improve’ express the same aim; order makes it more formal. Avoid ‘in order improving’ or ‘in order for improve’ — the to-infinitive is needed.",
"linkers:b2:7": "Contrast: <b>although + clause</b>. ‘Despite feeling ill’ (preposition + gerund) becomes ‘although he was not feeling well’ (conjunction + clause); ill becomes the opposite, not well. Avoid ‘although feeling’ without a subject — keep subject and verb.",
"linkers:b2:8": "Result: <b>this means + clause</b>. ‘Therefore, we will need more time’ states a consequence; ‘this means we will need’ does the same with a noun subject and the verb means. Avoid ‘this means to need’ — here mean takes a that-clause, not an infinitive.",
"linkers:b2:9": "Negative adverb inversion: <b>Not only + auxiliary + subject</b>. ‘Besides being tired, I was also hungry’ becomes ‘Not only was I tired, but I was also hungry’. Avoid ‘Not only I was tired’ — inversion is needed after Not only.",
"linkers:c1:0": "Concession: <b>No matter how + adjective + subject + verb</b>. ‘However hard she tried’ means the same as ‘no matter how hard she tried’. Avoid ‘no matter how hard did she try’ — no inversion is used here.",
"linkers:c1:1": "Cause: <b>owing to + noun phrase</b>. ‘Because very few tickets had been sold’ becomes ‘owing to poor ticket sales’, a noun phrase; few becomes the adjective poor. Avoid ‘owing to tickets were sold’ — a clause cannot follow owing to.",
"linkers:c1:2": "Condition: <b>on condition that + subject + verb</b>. ‘If you promise’ becomes ‘on condition that you promise’, a more formal conditional linker. Avoid ‘on condition of you pay’ — use that, then a clause with a present tense for future meaning.",
"linkers:c1:3": "Negative purpose: <b>for fear of + -ing</b>. ‘So that we wouldn’t miss’ becomes ‘for fear of missing’, where of is a preposition followed by a gerund. Avoid ‘for fear to miss’ and ‘for fear of not missing’ — the fear itself already carries the negative.",
"linkers:c1:4": "Contrast: <b>whereas + clause</b>. ‘Tom loves football, but his brother hates it’ keeps two opposite clauses, with whereas replacing but. Avoid ‘whereas but his brother’ — never use both linkers together.",
"linkers:c1:5": "<b>the reason why + clause</b>. ‘He resigned because of the scandal’ makes the scandal the subject: ‘The scandal was the reason why he resigned’. Avoid ‘the reason because he resigned’ — why or that follows reason.",
"linkers:c1:6": "<b>regardless of + noun phrase</b>. ‘Whatever the cost’ becomes ‘regardless of the cost’, where regardless is followed by of and a noun. Avoid ‘regardless the cost’ — of is required.",
"linkers:c1:7": "Addition: <b>In addition to + -ing</b>. ‘As well as being’ and ‘in addition to being’ both link two facts, and to is a preposition so -ing follows. Avoid ‘in addition to be’ — an infinitive cannot follow a preposition.",
"modals:b2:0": "Deduction about now: <b>must + base verb</b>. ‘I'm sure he is at home’ becomes ‘He must be at home’ — ‘must’ expresses strong certainty. Avoid ‘must to be’ or ‘must is’: after a modal, use the base form without ‘to’.",
"modals:b2:1": "Negative deduction about the past: <b>cannot have + past participle</b>. ‘It's impossible that she saw me’ becomes ‘She cannot have seen me’ — impossible, based on logic. Avoid ‘cannot saw’ and ‘can't have see’.",
"modals:b2:10": "Strong deduction about the past: <b>must have + past participle</b>. ‘I'm sure you dropped your wallet’ becomes ‘You must have dropped it’ — you are logically sure. Avoid ‘must dropped’ and ‘must drop’, which refers to the present.",
"modals:b2:11": "Regret about a past choice: <b>should not have + past participle</b> or <b>ought not to have + past participle</b>. ‘It was a bad idea to leave so late’ becomes ‘We shouldn't have left so late’. Avoid ‘shouldn't leave’, which is not about the past.",
"modals:b2:12": "Possibility about the past: <b>might have + past participle</b>. ‘There is a chance that she missed the train’ becomes ‘She might have missed the train’. Avoid ‘might missed’ and ‘might miss’, which looks to the future.",
"modals:b2:13": "Prohibition: <b>must not + base verb</b>. ‘It is forbidden to park here’ becomes ‘You must not park here’. ‘Are not allowed to’ is also correct. Avoid ‘don't have to park’, which means it is not necessary.",
"modals:b2:14": "Past general ability: <b>could + base verb</b>. ‘He had the ability to run fast’ becomes ‘He could run fast’; for a general skill in the past, ‘could’ is correct. Avoid ‘could ran’ — after a modal use the base form.",
"modals:b2:15": "Unnecessary action that was done: <b>need not have + past participle</b>. ‘It wasn't necessary to book, but we did’ becomes ‘We needn't have booked’, which means we booked without needing to. Avoid ‘didn't need to book’, which doesn't say whether we booked.",
"modals:b2:16": "Negative deduction about the past: <b>cannot have + past participle</b>. ‘I'm sure they haven't finished’ becomes ‘They can't have finished the work yet’, giving a logical conclusion. Avoid ‘can't finished’ and ‘mustn't have finished’.",
"modals:b2:17": "Advice through a second conditional: <b>If I were you, I would + base verb</b>. ‘I think you should see a doctor’ becomes ‘If I were you, I would see a doctor’. Use ‘were’ for the first person. Avoid ‘If I would be you’.",
"modals:b2:18": "Past obligation: <b>had to + base verb</b>. ‘We were obliged to wear’ becomes ‘We had to wear’. ‘Must’ has no past form, so ‘had to’ is used. Avoid ‘we must wear’ (present) and ‘had to wore’.",
"modals:b2:19": "Success with a noun phrase: <b>succeed in + -ing</b>. ‘I didn't manage to find’ becomes ‘I did not succeed in finding’ — ‘succeed’ needs ‘in’ and a gerund. Avoid ‘succeed to find’ — it takes ‘in’ + -ing, not an infinitive.",
"modals:b2:2": "Possibility about the past: <b>might have + past participle</b>. ‘Perhaps they forgot’ becomes ‘They might have forgotten’, expressing a past chance. Avoid ‘might forgot’ — after ‘have’ use the past participle ‘forgotten’.",
"modals:b2:3": "Criticism of a past action: <b>should not have + past participle</b>. ‘It was a mistake to talk to her’ becomes ‘You shouldn't have talked to her’ — you did it, and it was wrong. Avoid ‘shouldn't talk’, which is advice for now.",
"modals:b2:4": "Specific success in the past: <b>manage + to-infinitive</b>. ‘I wasn't able to finish’ becomes ‘I didn't manage to finish’, with ‘didn't’ carrying the tense. Avoid ‘didn't managed’ and ‘manage finishing’.",
"modals:b2:5": "Unnecessary action that was done: <b>need not have + past participle</b>. ‘It wasn't necessary to bring a gift’ becomes ‘You needn't have brought a gift’, meaning you brought one without need. Avoid ‘didn't need to bring’ for this meaning — it doesn't say what happened.",
"modals:b2:6": "Negative deduction about the past: <b>cannot / could not have + past participle</b>. ‘I am certain they didn't leave early’ becomes ‘They cannot have left early’. Avoid ‘cannot left’ and ‘must not have left’ — ‘must not have’ is much less common for deduction.",
"modals:b2:7": "Past necessity: <b>did + subject + have to + base verb</b>. ‘Was it necessary for you…?’ becomes ‘Did you have to work late?’ — ‘did’ makes the question, so ‘have’ stays in the base form. Avoid ‘Did you had to work’.",
"modals:b2:8": "Ability in a specific past event: <b>was able to + base verb</b>. ‘I succeeded in passing’ becomes ‘I was able to pass’. For one particular success, ‘could’ is not used in affirmative sentences. Avoid ‘I could pass’ and ‘was able to passed’.",
"modals:b2:9": "Advice: <b>ought to + base verb</b>. ‘It is a good idea to eat more vegetables’ becomes ‘You ought to eat more vegetables’ — the same meaning as ‘should’. Avoid ‘ought eat’ (missing ‘to’) and ‘ought to eating’.",
"modals:c1:0": "Past deduction with a negative idea: <b>cannot have been + adjective</b>. ‘I'm sure it wasn't easy’ becomes ‘It can't have been easy’; ‘been’ is needed because ‘be’ is in the perfect form. Avoid ‘can't have easy’ or ‘can't be easy’, which is present.",
"modals:c1:1": "Possibility about a past negative: <b>may not have + past participle</b>. ‘It's possible that he didn't receive’ becomes ‘He may not have received my message’. Avoid ‘may have not received’ in exams and ‘may not received’.",
"modals:c1:2": "Unnecessary action that was done: <b>need not have + past participle</b>. ‘There was no need for you to wait’ becomes ‘You needn't have waited’, meaning you did wait. Avoid ‘You needn't wait’ (present) and ‘needn't have wait’.",
"modals:c1:3": "Fixed expression: <b>might as well + base verb</b>. ‘There's nothing else to do, so we should go home’ becomes ‘We might as well go home’ — no better alternative exists. Avoid ‘might as well to go’ — no ‘to’ after ‘as well’.",
"modals:c1:4": "Criticism of a past action: <b>ought not to have + past participle</b>. ‘It was wrong of you to shout’ becomes ‘You ought not to have shouted’ — you shouted and it was wrong. Avoid ‘ought not shout’, which gives present advice.",
"modals:c1:5": "Strong deduction about the past: <b>must have + past participle</b>. ‘I bet they've got lost’ becomes ‘They must have got lost’ — ‘must have’ expresses near-certainty. Avoid ‘must be lost’ with a different meaning, and ‘must have get’.",
"modals:c1:6": "Possibility about a past action in progress: <b>could have been + -ing</b>. ‘Perhaps she was telling the truth’ becomes ‘She could have been telling the truth’, keeping the continuous idea. Avoid ‘could have told’, which means something different.",
"modals:c1:7": "Negative deduction about the past: <b>cannot have + past participle</b>. ‘I'm sure he didn't realise’ becomes ‘He can't have realised’ — logically impossible. Avoid ‘must not have realised’, which is unnatural for deduction, and ‘can't realised’.",
"passive:b2:0": "Causative for something bad that happened to you: <b>have + object + past participle</b>. ‘Someone stole my bike’ becomes ‘I had my bike stolen’ — the subject suffers the action, someone else does it. Avoid ‘I had stolen my bike’, which means you stole it.",
"passive:b2:1": "Present continuous causative: <b>is/are having + object + past participle</b>. ‘They are repairing his car’ becomes ‘He is having his car repaired’ — he arranges for someone else to do the job. Avoid ‘is having repaired his car’: the object goes before the participle.",
"passive:b2:10": "Passive of ‘going to’: <b>is going to + be + past participle</b>. ‘They are going to demolish that factory’ becomes ‘That factory is going to be pulled down’; with PULLED the phrasal verb ‘pull down’ replaces ‘demolish’. Avoid ‘going to pulled’ — use ‘be’ before the participle.",
"passive:b2:11": "Impersonal passive with a future idea: <b>is expected + to-infinitive</b>. ‘People expect that the president will resign’ becomes ‘The president is expected to resign’, with the president as the new subject. Avoid ‘is expected will resign’ — the verb after ‘expected’ takes ‘to’.",
"passive:b2:12": "Causative with ‘have/get’: <b>had + object + past participle</b>. ‘A designer made her dress’ becomes ‘She had her wedding dress made by a famous designer’ — she arranged it, and ‘by’ names the person who did it. Avoid ‘She made her dress’, which means she made it herself.",
"passive:b2:13": "Past perfect passive: <b>had + not + been + past participle</b>. ‘No one had told me’ becomes ‘I had not been informed’, with ‘I’ as the subject. ‘Inform’ needs a preposition before the topic: ‘about’ or ‘of’. Avoid ‘informed the meeting’ without it.",
"passive:b2:14": "Present simple passive for a routine: <b>is/are + past participle</b> (or ‘gets cleaned’ informally). ‘Someone cleans the office’ becomes ‘The office is cleaned’, since the cleaner is unknown or unimportant. Avoid ‘is clean’, which describes a state, not the action.",
"passive:b2:15": "Future perfect passive: <b>will have + been + past participle</b>. ‘They will have completed the project’ becomes ‘The project will have been completed’. The key word BEEN is the part learners forget. Avoid ‘will have completed’, which makes the project the doer.",
"passive:b2:16": "Causative after ‘need to’: <b>have/get + object + past participle</b>. The speaker needs someone else to check his eyes, so ‘my eyesight’ goes between the verb and ‘tested’. Avoid ‘have tested my eyesight’, which means you carry out the test yourself.",
"passive:b2:17": "Impersonal passive in the past: <b>was thought + to be</b>. ‘People thought the painting was a fake’ becomes ‘The painting was thought to be a fake’ — same time, so a simple infinitive. Avoid ‘was thought to have been’ here, which would place the fake status earlier.",
"passive:b2:18": "Causative of something you arrange: <b>having + object + past participle</b>. ‘We are employing a gardener to cut the grass’ becomes ‘We are having the grass cut by a gardener’ — we are not cutting it ourselves. Avoid ‘having cut the grass’, which has no clear causative meaning.",
"passive:b2:19": "Present perfect passive: <b>has/have + not + been + past participle</b>. ‘They haven't delivered the furniture’ becomes ‘The furniture has not been delivered’ — the furniture is the subject and ‘furniture’ is uncountable, so ‘has’. Avoid ‘have not delivered’.",
"passive:b2:2": "Impersonal passive with a report verb: <b>subject + is said + to-infinitive</b>. ‘People say that the band is breaking up’ becomes ‘The band is said to be breaking up’, so ‘the band’ becomes the subject. Avoid ‘is said that’ after a new subject.",
"passive:b2:3": "Report verb in the passive about an earlier event: <b>is believed + to have + past participle</b>. The escape happened before the belief, so the perfect infinitive ‘to have escaped’ is needed. Avoid ‘is believed to escape’, which sounds like a present or future action.",
"passive:b2:4": "Passive of a verb + object + to-infinitive: <b>were told + to + infinitive</b>. The object ‘us’ becomes the subject ‘We’, and the instruction keeps its ‘to open’ form. Avoid ‘were told open’ — keep ‘to’ after the passive verb.",
"passive:b2:5": "Present perfect passive: <b>has/have + been + past participle</b>. ‘Nobody has cleaned’ becomes ‘has not been cleaned’: the room is now the subject and the negative sits in the verb. Avoid ‘has not cleaned’, which makes the room the doer.",
"passive:b2:6": "Causative with ‘get’ in a future plan: <b>am getting + object + past participle</b>. ‘A professional is going to paint my portrait’ becomes ‘I am getting my portrait painted’ — the present continuous often expresses an arrangement. Avoid ‘getting painted my portrait’.",
"passive:b2:7": "Modal passive: <b>modal + be + past participle</b>. ‘You must finish the report’ becomes ‘The report must be finished by Friday’, so the object becomes the subject. Avoid ‘must finished’ or ‘must been finished’ — after a modal use the base form ‘be’.",
"passive:b2:8": "Passive with two objects: <b>was/were + not + given</b>. In ‘They didn't give me the information’ the person becomes the subject: ‘I was not given the information’. Avoid ‘I didn't given’ — the auxiliary ‘was’ already carries the tense, and ‘given’ is the participle.",
"passive:b2:9": "Present continuous passive: <b>is/are being + past participle</b>. ‘Someone is building a new hospital’ becomes ‘A new hospital is being built’ — the action is in progress and the doer is unimportant. Avoid ‘is building’, which makes the hospital the builder.",
"passive:c1:0": "Passive report verb with a past event: <b>is thought + to have + past participle</b>. ‘It is thought that the fire was started’ becomes ‘The fire is thought to have been started’; the fire is the subject and the perfect infinitive passive marks the earlier time. Avoid ‘to be started’.",
"passive:c1:1": "Continuous passive infinitive: <b>to be + being + past participle</b>. The investigation is in progress, so ‘is said to be investigating’ becomes ‘to be being investigated’, with the incident as the subject. Avoid ‘to be investigated’, which loses the idea that it is happening now.",
"passive:c1:2": "Idiom in the passive: <b>keep someone in the dark</b> = not tell someone anything. ‘Nobody told the residents anything’ becomes ‘The residents were kept in the dark about…’, using the past passive ‘were kept’. Avoid ‘were keep’ or ‘kept the dark’.",
"passive:c1:3": "Modal of deduction in the passive (past): <b>must have + been + past participle</b>. ‘Someone must have stolen the documents’ becomes ‘The documents must have been stolen’ — the documents become the subject. Avoid ‘must have stolen’, which makes the documents the thieves.",
"passive:c1:4": "Criticism of a past failure, in the passive: <b>should have + been + past participle</b>. ‘The council should have repaired the bridge’ becomes ‘The bridge should have been repaired’, so the bridge is the subject. Avoid ‘should have repaired’ with a subject that cannot do the repairing.",
"passive:c1:5": "Negative passive with a report verb: <b>is not expected + to-infinitive</b>. ‘Experts don't expect the economy to recover’ becomes ‘The economy is not expected to recover’; the object becomes the subject and keeps its infinitive. Avoid ‘doesn't expected’.",
"passive:c1:6": "Verb followed by a passive gerund: <b>resent + being + past participle</b>. ‘She resents it when people interrupt her’ becomes ‘She resents being interrupted’ — she is the one interrupted, and the doers disappear. Avoid ‘resents to be interrupted’: resent is followed by -ing.",
"passive:c1:7": "Report verb with a perfect infinitive: <b>is claimed + to have + past participle</b>. ‘It is claimed that the minister knew nothing’ becomes ‘The minister is claimed to have known nothing’; the knowing was before the claim. Avoid ‘is claimed to know’.",
"phrasal:b2:0": "<b>Put up with</b> = tolerate. It is a three-part phrasal verb, so the object goes after the whole verb: ‘put up with this noise’. Avoid ‘put up this noise with’ — three-part phrasal verbs cannot be separated.",
"phrasal:b2:1": "<b>Run out of</b> + noun = have no more of something. Here ‘no more milk left’ becomes ‘have run out of milk’; the gap is the three-part phrasal verb. Avoid ‘run out milk’ — the preposition <b>of</b> is required before the noun.",
"phrasal:b2:10": "<b>Bear / keep sth in mind</b> = remember to consider it. The original ‘think about’ becomes the fixed phrase ‘bear the cost in mind’; the object goes between bear and in mind. Avoid ‘bear in the mind’ — there is no article before mind.",
"phrasal:b2:11": "<b>Look up to</b> sb = admire and respect them. It is a three-part phrasal verb, so the object follows the whole verb: ‘look up to my grandfather’. Avoid confusing it with ‘look up’ (search for information) or ‘look down on’ (despise).",
"phrasal:b2:12": "<b>Put off</b> = postpone. The original has ‘they’ as subject, but the second sentence starts with ‘the match’, so you need a passive: <b>was put off</b> (be + past participle). Avoid ‘was put out’ (extinguished) or ‘put off’ without was.",
"phrasal:b2:13": "<b>Keep an eye on</b> = watch / look after. ‘Watch’ is replaced by this fixed phrase, followed by the object: ‘keep an eye on my bag’. The article an and the preposition on are both required. Avoid ‘keep eyes on’ or ‘keep an eye at’.",
"phrasal:b2:14": "<b>Get over</b> = recover from (an illness, a shock, a disappointment). ‘Recovered from’ becomes the past form ‘got over’ plus the object: ‘got over his illness’. Avoid ‘got over from’ — over already includes the idea of from.",
"phrasal:b2:15": "<b>Come across</b> = find or meet by chance. ‘Accidentally found’ is expressed by the verb itself: ‘came across this old photo’. It is inseparable, so the object follows across. Avoid ‘came across to’ or ‘came across with’.",
"phrasal:b2:16": "<b>Be up to</b> sb = be someone’s decision or responsibility. ‘It’s your decision’ becomes ‘It’s up to you’, with an object pronoun (you), not ‘your’. Avoid ‘It’s up to your’ — after to you need me, you, him, her, etc.",
"phrasal:b2:17": "<b>Take part in</b> = participate in. The verb participate is replaced by the collocation take + part + <b>in</b>, followed by a noun: ‘take part in the competition’. Avoid ‘take part of’ or ‘take a part in’ — no article, and the preposition is in.",
"phrasal:b2:18": "<b>Take up</b> + noun = begin a new hobby, sport or activity. ‘Started doing yoga’ becomes ‘took up yoga’; the past of take is irregular (took). Avoid ‘took up to do yoga’ — take up is followed by a noun, not an infinitive.",
"phrasal:b2:19": "<b>Cut down on</b> (or ‘cut back on’) = reduce the amount of something. It is a three-part phrasal verb, so on must be followed by the noun or -ing: ‘cut down on sugar’. Avoid ‘cut down’ alone with this meaning, which is incomplete here.",
"phrasal:b2:2": "<b>Come up with</b> = think of / invent (an idea, excuse, solution). The original verb ‘invented’ is replaced by a three-part phrasal verb in the past: ‘came up with a brilliant excuse’. Avoid ‘came up a excuse’ — keep with and the article.",
"phrasal:b2:3": "<b>Turn down</b> = reject (an offer, invitation, application). It is separable: ‘turned down their job offer’ or ‘turned their job offer down’, but a pronoun must go in the middle: ‘turned it down’. Avoid ‘turned off’, which means switch off.",
"phrasal:b2:4": "<b>Call off</b> = cancel. The original is passive (‘was cancelled’), so the answer keeps the passive: <b>was called off</b> (past simple passive of the phrasal verb). Avoid ‘called off’ without ‘was’ — the meeting does not cancel itself.",
"phrasal:b2:5": "<b>Get on with</b> sb = have a good relationship with them (also ‘get along with’). It is a three-part phrasal verb, followed directly by the person. ‘Doesn’t have a good relationship’ becomes the negative ‘doesn’t get on with’. Avoid ‘get on to’ or ‘get with’.",
"phrasal:b2:6": "<b>Blame sb for + -ing</b>: ‘Don’t blame me for being late.’ The original says ‘it’s not my fault that we are late’; the verb blame needs the person first, then for and a gerund. Avoid ‘blame me to be late’ or ‘blame me of being late’.",
"phrasal:b2:7": "<b>On purpose</b> = deliberately, intentionally. The fixed phrase follows the object: ‘I didn’t break the vase on purpose’ = ‘I didn’t mean to break it’. Avoid ‘with purpose’ or ‘by purpose’ — the preposition must be <b>on</b>.",
"phrasal:b2:8": "<b>Take after</b> sb = resemble a family member in looks or character. It is inseparable, followed by the person: ‘takes after her mother’. Note the third-person -s on takes. Avoid ‘takes like’ or ‘takes after to’ — no extra preposition.",
"phrasal:b2:9": "<b>Dawn on</b> sb = become clear to someone. The subject changes from ‘he’ to ‘it’ (dummy subject), and he becomes the object: ‘It dawned on him what had happened.’ Avoid ‘dawned to him’ — the preposition must be <b>on</b>.",
"phrasal:c1:0": "<b>Take sb by surprise</b> = surprise them. The original ‘came as a surprise to everyone’ has a different subject structure; here ‘news’ is subject and the person is the object: ‘took everyone completely by surprise’. Avoid ‘took to surprise’ — the preposition is <b>by</b>.",
"phrasal:c1:1": "<b>Cut back on</b> = reduce spending or consumption. ‘Reduce’ becomes a three-part phrasal verb followed by the noun: ‘cut back on its costs’. Avoid ‘cut back’ without on when an object follows, and avoid ‘cut back of’.",
"phrasal:c1:2": "<b>At a loss</b> = not knowing what to do or say. ‘Couldn’t think of an answer’ becomes ‘was at a loss for an answer’ (or ‘at a loss to find’). Avoid ‘in a loss’ or ‘at loss’ — the fixed phrase needs <b>at a loss</b>.",
"phrasal:c1:3": "<b>Take sth into account</b> = consider it when deciding. The verb consider is replaced by this collocation: ‘take his age into account’ (or ‘take into account his age’). Avoid ‘take in account’ or ‘take account’ without the preposition into.",
"phrasal:c1:4": "<b>Take notice of</b> = pay attention to. ‘Paid no attention’ becomes ‘took no notice of’; after the negative subject ‘Nobody’ use <b>any</b>: ‘Nobody took any notice of her warnings’. Avoid ‘took notice to’ — the preposition is of.",
"phrasal:c1:5": "<b>Have sth in common</b> = share characteristics. ‘Very similar’ becomes ‘have a lot in common’; the subject (the two companies) is plural, so use have. Avoid ‘have much common’ or ‘have in common a lot’ — keep the order have + quantity + in common.",
"phrasal:c1:6": "<b>Take the trouble to</b> + infinitive = make the effort to do something. ‘Made no effort’ becomes the negative ‘didn’t take the trouble to help’. Avoid ‘take the trouble of help’ — use the to-infinitive, and keep the definite article <b>the</b>.",
"phrasal:c1:7": "<b>Get the hang of</b> sth = learn how to do or use it. ‘Learn how to use’ becomes ‘get the hang of’ plus a noun or -ing: ‘get the hang of the new system’. Avoid ‘get hang of’ — the article the and the preposition of are fixed.",
"reported:b2:0": "Reporting verb + gerund: <b>deny + -ing</b>. ‘I didn't break’ becomes ‘Tom denied breaking’ (or ‘having broken’), with no ‘not’ because ‘deny’ is already negative. Avoid ‘denied to break’ — deny is followed by -ing.",
"reported:b2:1": "Reporting verb of suggestion: <b>suggest + -ing</b> or <b>suggest (that) + subject + base verb</b>. ‘Why don't we go?’ becomes ‘suggested going’. Avoid ‘suggested to go’ and ‘suggested me to go’ — suggest never takes an object + to-infinitive.",
"reported:b2:10": "Reporting verb + gerund: <b>deny + having + past participle</b>. ‘I've never seen this man’ becomes ‘denied having (ever) seen that man’; the perfect gerund shows an earlier time, and ‘this’ becomes ‘that’. Avoid ‘denied to see’.",
"reported:b2:11": "Reporting a suggestion: <b>suggest/propose + -ing</b>. ‘Let's throw a party’ becomes ‘suggested throwing a surprise party’, including the speaker in the action. Avoid ‘suggested to throw’ — the verb takes a gerund, not an infinitive.",
"reported:b2:12": "Reporting a warning: <b>warn + object + not + to-infinitive</b>. ‘Don't swim’ becomes ‘warned us not to swim’, with the negative before ‘to’. Avoid ‘warned us to not swim’ and ‘warned us do not swim’.",
"reported:b2:13": "Reporting a promise: <b>promise + to-infinitive</b>. ‘I will pay you back’ becomes ‘promised to pay me back’; ‘you’ becomes ‘me’ and the future is replaced by the infinitive. Avoid ‘promised paying’ and ‘promised I will pay’.",
"reported:b2:14": "Reported request as a question: <b>asked + if/whether + subject + could</b>. ‘Can you help me?’ becomes ‘if I could help him’ — ‘can’ goes back to ‘could’ and the pronouns change. Avoid ‘if could I help’.",
"reported:b2:15": "Reporting an admission: <b>admit + -ing</b> or <b>admit + having + past participle</b>. ‘I broke the computer’ becomes ‘admitted breaking’ — a gerund replaces the past tense. Avoid ‘admitted to break’ — admit is followed by -ing.",
"reported:b2:16": "Reporting an invitation: <b>invite + object + to-infinitive</b>. ‘Would you like to come?’ becomes ‘invited him to come’, with the person directly after the verb. Avoid ‘invited to him come’ or ‘invited him coming’.",
"reported:b2:17": "Reporting an insistence: <b>insist on + object/possessive + -ing</b> or <b>insist (that) + subject + base verb</b>. ‘You must stay’ becomes ‘insisted on us staying’. Avoid ‘insisted to stay’ — insist needs ‘on’ or a that-clause.",
"reported:b2:18": "Reported wh-question: <b>asked + question word + subject + verb</b>. ‘Who does this jacket belong to?’ becomes ‘whose jacket it was’ — statement order, and the question verb goes back to ‘was’. Avoid ‘whose jacket was it’.",
"reported:b2:19": "Negative intention: <b>have no intention of + -ing</b>. ‘I didn't mean to delete the file’ becomes ‘no intention of deleting’ — after the preposition ‘of’ a gerund is used. Avoid ‘no intention to delete’ here; it must follow ‘of’.",
"reported:b2:2": "Apologising needs a preposition: <b>apologise for + -ing</b>. ‘I'm sorry I arrived late’ becomes ‘apologised for arriving’ (or ‘being late’), with the same subject. Avoid ‘apologised to arrive’ or ‘apologised arriving’.",
"reported:b2:3": "Reporting verb with a preposition: <b>accuse someone of + -ing</b>. ‘You stole my wallet!’ becomes ‘accused the boy of stealing’, with the person accused as the object. Avoid ‘accused him for stealing’ — the preposition is ‘of’.",
"reported:b2:4": "Reporting verb + object + to-infinitive: <b>remind + someone + to + base verb</b>. ‘Don't forget to buy milk’ becomes ‘reminded him to buy milk’. Avoid ‘reminded him buy’ and ‘reminded to him’ — the person comes directly after the verb.",
"reported:b2:5": "Reporting verb of offering: <b>offer + to-infinitive</b>. ‘I'll carry your bag’ becomes ‘offered to carry’, with the same subject doing it. Avoid ‘offered carrying’ and ‘offered that he will carry’.",
"reported:b2:6": "Reporting a refusal: <b>refuse + to-infinitive</b>. ‘No, I won't do it’ becomes ‘refused to do it’ — refuse is already negative. Avoid ‘refused not to do’ and ‘refused doing’.",
"reported:b2:7": "Reported yes/no question: <b>asked + whether/if + subject + verb</b>. ‘Are you going?’ becomes ‘whether I was going’ — the tense moves back one step and the word order is a statement. Avoid ‘whether was I going’.",
"reported:b2:8": "Indirect question: <b>know + question word + subject + verb</b>. ‘Where is the station?’ becomes ‘know where the station was’ — the word order becomes a statement and the verb goes back one tense. Avoid ‘where was the station’.",
"reported:b2:9": "Reporting advice: <b>advise + object + to-infinitive</b>. ‘You should apply’ becomes ‘advised me to apply’; the modal ‘should’ is replaced by the reporting verb. Avoid ‘advised me apply’ and ‘advised to apply’ without an object.",
"reported:c1:0": "Reported prohibition: <b>were not permitted + to-infinitive</b>. ‘You can't park here’ becomes ‘we were not permitted to park there’; ‘can't’ goes back to a passive form and ‘here’ becomes ‘there’. Avoid ‘permitted park’ — ‘permit’ needs ‘to’.",
"reported:c1:1": "Reporting verb of blame: <b>blame someone for + -ing</b>. ‘It was Tom who broke it’ becomes ‘blamed Tom for breaking it’. Avoid ‘blamed Tom of’ (that is ‘accused of’) and ‘blamed him to break’.",
"reported:c1:2": "Reporting a strong recommendation: <b>urge + object + to-infinitive</b>. ‘You really must try’ becomes ‘urged us to try’, where ‘urged’ carries the force of ‘really must’. Avoid ‘urged to us try’ or ‘urged that us try’.",
"reported:c1:3": "Reporting congratulations: <b>congratulate someone on + -ing</b>. ‘Well done on winning’ becomes ‘congratulated her on winning’. Avoid ‘congratulated her for winning’ — the standard preposition is ‘on’ — and ‘to win’.",
"reported:c1:4": "Reporting a promise: <b>assure + object + (that) + subject + would</b>. ‘I'll definitely be there’ becomes ‘assured us that he would’; ‘will’ goes back to ‘would’. Avoid ‘assured that he will’ without an object — assure needs one.",
"reported:c1:5": "Negative promise: <b>promise + not + to-infinitive</b>. ‘I won't tell anyone’ becomes ‘promised not to tell anyone’, with ‘not’ placed before ‘to’. Avoid ‘promised to not tell’ in formal exams and ‘promised not telling’.",
"reported:c1:6": "Reporting a recommendation: <b>recommend (that) + subject + (should) + base verb</b>. ‘You should see’ becomes ‘recommended that I see’ — the subjunctive keeps the base form. Avoid ‘recommended that I to see’.",
"reported:c1:7": "Reporting a denial: <b>deny + -ing / having + past participle</b>. ‘I wasn't anywhere near’ becomes ‘denied being near’ — the idea of ‘not’ is in the verb ‘deny’. Avoid ‘denied not being’ and ‘denied to be’.",
"tenses:b2:0": "Present perfect with <b>for + period</b>. ‘Last saw him two years ago’ becomes ‘have not seen him for two years’: a negative present perfect describes the period up to now. Avoid ‘didn’t see him for two years’ — the gap needs have, not did.",
"tenses:b2:1": "Present perfect continuous with <b>since + point in time</b>. ‘Started working here in 2015’ is still true now, so ‘has been working here since 2015’. Avoid ‘is working since’ or ‘worked since’ — since needs a perfect tense.",
"tenses:b2:10": "Present perfect with <b>for ages</b>. ‘It’s ages since I went’ means my last visit was long ago, so ‘I haven’t been to the theater for ages’. Been is used for completed visits. Avoid ‘I haven’t gone’ — gone means someone has not returned.",
"tenses:b2:11": "Present perfect continuous: <b>have been + -ing + for</b>. ‘Started reading three weeks ago’ is still in progress, so ‘have been reading… for three weeks’. Avoid ‘am reading for’ or ‘read for’ — for needs a perfect tense.",
"tenses:b2:12": "Present perfect after a superlative: <b>the + superlative + he has ever + past participle</b>. ‘Never driven such a fast car’ means no car was faster, so ‘the fastest car he has ever driven’. Avoid ‘ever drove’ — a life experience up to now takes the present perfect.",
"tenses:b2:13": "Past perfect: <b>had already started</b>. The match began before I arrived, so the earlier event takes the past perfect and the arrival takes the past simple after by the time. Avoid ‘has already started’ — the present perfect ignores the past time reference.",
"tenses:b2:14": "Present continuous with <b>always</b> for an annoying habit: <b>is always taking</b>. ‘Constantly taking’ expresses irritation about a repeated action. Always goes between be and the -ing form. Avoid ‘always takes’ — it sounds neutral, not annoyed.",
"tenses:b2:15": "Past habit: <b>used to + infinitive</b>. ‘Would play every day’ describes a repeated activity in the past, which can also be expressed as ‘used to play’. Avoid ‘used to playing’ and ‘was used to play’ — both change the meaning.",
"tenses:b2:16": "<b>mean to + infinitive</b>. ‘I’m sure he didn’t do it on purpose’ becomes ‘he didn’t mean to do it’, since mean (intend) is followed by a to-infinitive. Avoid ‘didn’t meant’ — after did not the verb stays in base form.",
"tenses:b2:17": "Causative: <b>have + object + past participle</b>. ‘It’s two months since I last had a haircut’ means I haven’t had one for two months. Someone else does it, so ‘haven’t had my hair cut’. Avoid ‘haven’t cut my hair’, which means I do it myself.",
"tenses:b2:18": "Past perfect continuous: <b>had been + -ing + for</b>. The waiting continued up to the moment the bus arrived, so we use ‘had been waiting for an hour’. Avoid ‘had waited’ — the key word BEEN needs the continuous aspect.",
"tenses:b2:19": "Past perfect with <b>just</b>. My arrival came immediately before the phone rang, so the earlier action takes ‘had just arrived’ and the later one the past simple. Just goes between had and the participle. Avoid ‘I just arrived when’ — the sequence is less clear.",
"tenses:b2:2": "Question about time elapsed: <b>How long ago + did + subject + base verb</b>. ‘When did you buy’ asks for the time, and how long ago asks for the same information with a past simple. Avoid ‘how long ago did you bought’ — the main verb stays in base form.",
"tenses:b2:3": "<b>the first time + present perfect</b>. ‘I have never eaten sushi before’ becomes ‘the first time I have eaten sushi’, since this experience is new. Avoid ‘the first time I never eat’ — never has no place here.",
"tenses:b2:4": "Present perfect with <b>not… yet</b>. ‘Still doing’ means the action isn’t complete, so ‘has not finished… yet’. Yet goes at the end of a negative sentence. Avoid ‘doesn’t finish yet’ or ‘didn’t finished’ — use has + past participle.",
"tenses:b2:5": "<b>used to + infinitive</b> for a past habit that has stopped. ‘Usually walked… as a child’ becomes ‘used to walk’. Avoid ‘used to walking’ — that means ‘was accustomed to’ — and ‘used to walked’, since used to is followed by a base form.",
"tenses:b2:6": "Time clause: <b>as soon as + past simple</b>. ‘The moment she heard’ means immediately after, which is the same as ‘as soon as she heard’. Avoid ‘as soon she heard’ — as is needed twice.",
"tenses:b2:7": "<b>while + past continuous</b> for a longer action in progress. ‘During the film’ (noun) becomes ‘while I was watching the film’ (clause), since while needs subject + verb. Avoid ‘while the film’ — only during can be followed by a noun alone.",
"tenses:b2:8": "Past perfect: <b>had already + past participle</b>. The train’s departure happened before we arrived, so the earlier action takes the past perfect and the arrival takes the past simple. Avoid ‘the train already left’ — it does not show the sequence as clearly.",
"tenses:b2:9": "Present perfect with <b>not yet</b>. ‘Still painting’ means the job isn’t finished, so ‘have not yet finished’ painting. Yet may come before the participle. Avoid ‘haven’t finish’ and ‘didn’t yet’ — use have + past participle.",
"tenses:c1:0": "Future perfect continuous: <b>will have been + -ing</b>. ‘Will have worked for a decade’ becomes ‘will have been working’, stressing a duration that continues up to a future point. Avoid ‘will be working’ — it does not look back from the future time.",
"tenses:c1:1": "<b>It’s + period + since + past simple</b>. ‘Haven’t had a holiday for over two years’ equals ‘it’s over two years since we last had a holiday’. Last goes before the past-simple verb. Avoid ‘since we have last had’ — the verb after since is past simple here.",
"tenses:c1:2": "Near future: <b>be on the point of + -ing</b>. ‘About to take off’ means imminent action, expressed more formally as ‘on the point of taking off’. Point is a noun, so a gerund follows of. Avoid ‘on the point to take’ — of requires -ing.",
"tenses:c1:3": "Negative adverb in the past perfect: <b>had hardly + past participle</b>. ‘Had only just sat down’ means barely seated, so ‘had hardly sat down’. Hardly sits between had and the participle. Avoid ‘had hardly not sat’ — hardly is already negative.",
"tenses:c1:4": "<b>be accustomed to + -ing</b>. ‘Not used to getting up’ means the same as ‘not accustomed to getting up’. To is a preposition, so a gerund follows. Avoid ‘not accustomed to get up’ — an infinitive is wrong after a preposition.",
"tenses:c1:5": "Question about duration: <b>How long + have you + been + -ing</b>. ‘When did you start learning’ becomes ‘how long have you been learning’, since the learning continues now. Avoid ‘how long did you learn’ — it suggests the learning has finished.",
"tenses:c1:6": "Present perfect negative: <b>has not spoken to + for + period</b>. ‘Last spoke a year ago’ means no talking since then, so ‘has not spoken to his brother for a year’. Avoid ‘didn’t speak… for a year’ — a period up to now needs the present perfect.",
"tenses:c1:7": "<b>be in the middle of + -ing</b>. ‘Was cooking when’ shows an action in progress interrupted by another, and in the middle of is a prepositional phrase followed by a gerund. Avoid ‘in the middle of cook’ or ‘in the middle to cook’.",
"verbpatterns:b2:0": "Verb + preposition + gerund: <b>look forward to + -ing</b>. ‘Can’t wait to go’ expresses eager anticipation, and in this phrase to is a preposition, so it takes -ing. Avoid ‘looking forward to go’ — an infinitive is incorrect after a preposition.",
"verbpatterns:b2:1": "<b>have trouble + -ing</b>. ‘Finds it difficult to wake up’ becomes ‘has trouble waking up’: the noun trouble is followed by a gerund. Avoid ‘has trouble to wake up’ — no infinitive follows trouble.",
"verbpatterns:b2:10": "Passive of force: <b>be forced to + infinitive</b>. ‘My parents made me do’ shifts the focus to me: ‘I was forced to do’. Avoid ‘I was forced do’ — unlike active make + bare infinitive, force needs to.",
"verbpatterns:b2:11": "<b>advise + object + against + -ing</b>. ‘Advise you not to trust’ becomes ‘advise you against trusting’; against is a preposition, so a gerund follows. Avoid ‘advise you against to trust’ — no infinitive after a preposition.",
"verbpatterns:b2:12": "<b>apologise (to someone) for + -ing</b>. ‘I’m sorry that I lost’ becomes a verb pattern: apologise takes the preposition for, then a gerund. Avoid ‘apologise to lose’ and ‘apologise that I lost’.",
"verbpatterns:b2:13": "<b>It’s no use + -ing</b>. ‘Completely pointless to try’ becomes ‘no use trying’, a fixed phrase followed by a gerund. Avoid ‘no use to try’ — the infinitive does not follow this expression.",
"verbpatterns:b2:14": "<b>can’t stand + -ing</b>. ‘Really hate doing’ and ‘cannot stand doing’ both express strong dislike; stand (meaning tolerate) is followed by a gerund. Avoid ‘can’t stand to doing’ — and remember the negative: ‘stand doing’ alone would mean the opposite.",
"verbpatterns:b2:15": "Polite request: <b>Do you mind if + subject + present simple</b>. ‘Will you let me use’ becomes a request for permission; after if, use the present tense, not will. Avoid ‘Do you mind if I will use’.",
"verbpatterns:b2:16": "<b>be used to + -ing</b>. ‘Not accustomed to driving’ means the same, and in this phrase to is a preposition, so the gerund follows. Avoid ‘not used to drive’ — that confuses it with ‘used to drive’ (a past habit).",
"verbpatterns:b2:17": "<b>find it + adjective + to-infinitive</b>. ‘It was difficult for me to understand’ moves the person to the subject: ‘I found it difficult to understand’. Avoid ‘I found difficult to understand’ — the object it is required.",
"verbpatterns:b2:18": "<b>It takes + person + time + to-infinitive</b>. ‘We spent hours trying’ becomes ‘It took us hours to solve’, with it as the subject and us as the object. Avoid ‘It took hours for solving’ — an infinitive follows, not a gerund.",
"verbpatterns:b2:19": "<b>promise (not) to + infinitive</b>. ‘Said she would definitely not be’ becomes ‘promised not to be’, with the negative before to. Avoid ‘promised to not be’ or ‘promised not being’ — promise takes a to-infinitive.",
"verbpatterns:b2:2": "<b>There is no point (in) + -ing</b>. ‘It’s useless to argue’ means arguing has no purpose, so we use no point with a gerund (in is optional). Avoid ‘no point to argue’ — the infinitive is not used after this phrase.",
"verbpatterns:b2:3": "<b>succeed in + -ing</b>. ‘Managed to fix’ becomes ‘succeeded in fixing’ because succeed takes the preposition in, followed by a gerund. Avoid ‘succeeded to fix’ — manage takes an infinitive, but succeed does not.",
"verbpatterns:b2:4": "<b>would rather + bare infinitive</b>. ‘Would prefer to stay’ means the same as ‘would rather stay’, but rather drops the to. Avoid ‘would rather to stay’ or ‘would rather staying’ — neither to nor -ing follows rather.",
"verbpatterns:b2:5": "Passive of allow: <b>be allowed to + infinitive</b>. ‘They didn’t let me leave’ makes me the subject: ‘I was not allowed to leave’. Let has no natural passive, so we use allow instead. Avoid ‘I was not let leave’.",
"verbpatterns:b2:6": "Passive gerund after a feeling verb: <b>hate + being + past participle</b>. ‘People tell him what to do’ becomes ‘being told what to do’ because he is the receiver of the action. Avoid ‘hates telling’ — that would mean he dislikes telling others.",
"verbpatterns:b2:7": "Obligation: <b>must not + infinitive</b>. ‘It is important that you remember to bring’ becomes a prohibition on forgetting, so ‘must not forget to bring’. Avoid ‘forget bringing’ — that would refer to a past event, not a task still to do.",
"verbpatterns:b2:8": "Reporting verb: <b>warn + object + (not) to + infinitive</b>. The direct order ‘Don’t touch the glass’ becomes ‘warned us not to touch’; the negative goes before to. Avoid ‘warned us to not touch’ or ‘warned us for touching’.",
"verbpatterns:b2:9": "<b>not old enough to + infinitive</b>. ‘Too young’ expresses lack of the necessary age, so we use the opposite adjective with enough, which follows the adjective. Avoid ‘not enough old to watch’ — enough comes after the adjective.",
"verbpatterns:c1:0": "Reporting verb + perfect gerund: <b>admit + having + past participle</b>. ‘Confessed that he had made’ becomes ‘admitted having made’; the perfect gerund shows an earlier action. Avoid ‘admitted to make’ — admit takes -ing, not an infinitive.",
"verbpatterns:c1:1": "Regret about the past: <b>should have + past participle</b>. ‘I regret not taking’ and ‘I should have taken’ both criticise a past choice. Avoid ‘should had taken’ or ‘should took’ — should takes have + past participle.",
"verbpatterns:c1:2": "<b>be worth + -ing</b>. ‘Pointless to repair’ becomes ‘is not worth repairing’, with the bike as subject and an active gerund. Avoid ‘not worth to repair’ or ‘not worth being repaired’ — after worth we use a simple gerund.",
"verbpatterns:c1:3": "Passive of permit: <b>be permitted to + infinitive</b>. ‘Would not let anyone leave’ becomes ‘Nobody was permitted to leave’, with the affected people as subject and the doer in by the manager. Avoid ‘was permitted leave’ — to is needed.",
"verbpatterns:c1:4": "<b>recall + -ing</b>. ‘Don’t remember agreeing’ becomes ‘do not recall agreeing’; recall, like remember for a past action, takes a gerund. Avoid ‘recall to agree’ — the infinitive would suggest a future duty.",
"verbpatterns:c1:5": "<b>get used to + -ing</b>. ‘Is now accustomed to working’ becomes ‘has got used to working’, showing a change over time. Here to is a preposition, so -ing follows. Avoid ‘got used to work’ — that confuses it with ‘used to work’.",
"verbpatterns:c1:6": "<b>object to + -ing</b>. ‘Do you have any objection to my opening’ becomes the verb ‘object to me opening’; to is a preposition, so a gerund follows. Avoid ‘object to open’ — an infinitive cannot follow to here.",
"verbpatterns:c1:7": "Perfect gerund: <b>regret + having + past participle</b>. ‘It was a mistake to lend’ becomes ‘I regret having lent’, the perfect gerund marking the earlier action. Avoid ‘regret to lend’ — that means I am sorry to announce I am lending."
};
